WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.898
Threat modeling helps teams ask how a feature can be abused before the implementation hardens into production behavior.

2
00:00:07.898 --> 00:00:14.602
Without that step, teams may protect individual endpoints while leaving unsafe business flows intact.

3
00:00:14.602 --> 00:00:18.650
Security requirements translate risk into design constraints.

4
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:27.942
Requirements such as rate limits, approval gates, auditability, tenant isolation, and fraud controls should be visible before coding begins.

5
00:00:27.942 --> 00:00:38.960
Unsafe architectural assumptions often appear when services trust internal networks, client-side state, partner integrations, or sequence order more than they should.

6
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.933
Attackers routinely exploit those assumptions rather than a single defective line of code.

7
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:48.053
Insecure design is not fixed by scanning alone.

8
00:00:48.053 --> 00:00:58.606
It requires review of workflows, trust boundaries, misuse cases, control ownership, and the decisions that determine how the application is supposed to behave.

9
00:00:58.606 --> 00:01:02.588
Use a secure SDLC with security requirements at design time.

10
00:01:02.588 --> 00:01:06.836
Run threat modeling for critical workflows and trust boundaries.

11
00:01:06.836 --> 00:01:10.818
Perform security architecture reviews before major releases.

12
00:01:10.818 --> 00:01:21.371
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SA-3 - System Development Life Cycle; SA-8 - Security and Privacy Engineering Principles; RA-3 - Risk Assessment.

